The ČSSD doesn't know voldemorta or confectionery in Prague
According to ČSSD, the new councillors of the ČSSD will apparently have to overcome a certain language barrier to understand their old-fashioned ODS colleagues in the Prague City Council.
Aktuálně.cz has "tested the members of the new ČSSD board", how they know the notorious slang, which arose at the town hall during the last years, when Prague was ruled by the current Coalition partners.
The encoded dictionary, which the Prague political elite introduced during the previous era of Mayor Pavel Bém, describes some of the most well-known figures, situations and cases in the metropolis.
Surpruviously, it turned out that the new councilwomen do not fully comprehension.
At least that's what they say.
"Who is Voldemort?"
"I really don't know."
"I'm more of a rookie in European politics," Lukáš Kaucký, a councillor's councillor for culture, responded to a test of the "got-gross" glossary.
And although it is a political veterinarian, councillor Karel Březina reacted similarly.
For example, the term "golf hole".
"It's the only expression you're presenting, which I sometimes use."
"Of course, I don't generally recognize slang."
From the glossary, whose terms Aktuálně.cz published in June this year before the ODS electoral congregation, the editorial board selected six examples, mainly those that are notoriously associated with the Prague City Hall.
Socialists should have explained the terms "Voldemort", "the largest golf hole in Prague", "go to the confectionery", "bull neck", "betrayal of the capital city" and "please".
The trio from the councillors agreed with the quiz, the fourth - Antonín Weinert, who is supposed to head the Prague education system in the future - he first promised an interview, but later he did not answer the phone.
Fifth Councilman Petr Dolínek dismissed the test.
"I don't deal with these things at all, and I won't judge how the Democrats have fun."
"I'm now figuring out how we're going to understand each other as part of our programs," he responded.
As it has already been said, councillor Lukáš Kaucký would have reportedly distinguished in the headline that we are talking about an influential businesswoman and a tribute of the ex-prime minister Bém Roman Janoušek.
"Voldemort" Kaucký doesn't mean anything, and the only date he correctly guessed in rehearsal is "the largest golf hole in Prague".
"It's Blanka tunnel," Kaucký says, and immediately adds: "But I'm more familiar with the newspapers."
"I'm going to have to pay for a language course," he jokes.
Not only the matador Karel Březina, but the environmental councillor Lukáš Plachý is deciphering the golf hole as a Blanka tunnel.
The two Social-Democrat then know who voldemort really is, and Lukas Plachý is also familiar with where to find this character - he suspects the meaning of the term "go to the confectionery".
"I think it means to go to a Prague company," is Plachý close to deciphering that it is the headquarters of Janoušek's companies.
Conforming with Březina, he recalls that he drew his knowledge from the newspaper rather than to use such a dictionary.
There is no similar glorious gloom of slang expressions in Social-Democracy, according to test participants.
"I've consulted with my colleagues, but it looks like we really don't have anything like that."
"We call each other names, sometimes abbreviations and corruption of names are used," says Petr Hulínský, the chairman of Prague's ČSSD, who is nicknamed Huld.
"Bříza told me at school, but I don't know if my colleagues call me like that," Karel Březina confesses his own nickname.
Newspaper Aktuálně.cz has tried the name and it works.
Weinert Councillor is a nickname "Igráček" for Grandpa's or less accompanied by Dědek, a representative for the party's party's party members, who drew his attention to fraud in the sponsorship of the party before the elections, has the nickname "Igráček" for some colleagues.
Nicknames are also the only thing the Social-Squads admit in the intra-partisan language.
Hulínský does not like to hear Hulda, but the daily Aktuálně.cz recorded another nickname of the Prague chairman.
"Doctor of baton law" recalls that Petr Hulínský won his title JUDr. at the Bratislava Police Academies.
The government wants to curtail the turor of "witches", has issued a pamphlet
Women professed in Burkina Faso have several common signs.
They belong rather to the older generation, they can not get involved in the work, for example, for health reasons, they are unmartyed and do not have children.
The one who "gets a witch" is sent into issuance, so as not to endanger the rest of the village.
"Men rarely this fate can happen to this fate."
“Not even young healthy women are sworn because they help at work and are giving birth to children,” the nucleus, who in the capital of the West African state of Burkina Faso takes care of more than a one-hundred-wielding house who have expelled their communions.
The local government has now decided to crack down on an outspoken "social measure".
Published an information brochure, which should help curb witch hunt.
There's a swagger.
"More more than 70 per cent of adults in Burkina Faso can't read," Die Standard said.
Charges of witchcraft is also common in other African countries.
Seventy-two-year-old Ammy Hemmah died last week in Ghana, which neighbours Burkina Faso, after three women and two men were tortured, poured kerosene and ignition.
The case was reported by the BBC.
Perpetrators say it was an unfortunate coincidence.
According to their version, an oil ignited on the woman, which is used for ritual anointing.
It flaunted after the group wanted "pulling the evil spirit out of the woman."
Isolation "witches" is supported by people regardless of the level of educational attainment.
Der Standard cited Forty-two-year-old Burkina business-in-manufacturer Faso: “Human-holiers of human souls have always posed a danger.”
While in Burkina Faso is trying to alleviate a witch-hunt, the president himself took part in the Gambia last year.
"Gambian President Jammi after his aunt's death unleashed a large-scale witch-driven hunt that was supposed to participate in her death and try to kill even the president with black magic."
"To his aid, he encouraged shamamurs from near Guinea, who, accompanied by police officers, soldiers and personal security, forcibly diverted randomly selected men and women from Gambian villages to the president's farm in Canilai," Amnesty International said in a report.
On the farm, imprisoned people had to drink potion from herbs with hallucinogenic effects, which additionally caused intestinal and kidney problems.
Two incarcerated residents did not survive on the farm.
After international pressure, almost a thousand imprisoned were finally released.
Bakal's tenant: Drowning in the Stove, Signed Higher Lease
An elevator staffered man stepped from the door of the client center of the company RPG Byty "autobaron" Zdeněk Bakala, the largest private owner of rental apartments in the Czech Republic.
Beneath the arm he bore a file and in it new leases for three flats: his daughter, his daughter and his son, who is serving his sentence.
Deregregulation lifted the rent for all three.
Instead of 3500 per month, the son will pay CZK 4100 for an apartment 2+1 of the second category from next year.
"And it's not hot water there, it has to drown the petrma (thill-fuel stove)," the man points with his finger in his son's new covenant, on whose signature he has the power of attorney.
"But I've signed it," he explains half annoyed and half-resigned.
Civic association BytyOKD.cz, which described the new rental conditions as "incorrect and immordial", advising the tenants not to sign the new treaties and wait until December 7, when it will publish the instructions on how to proceed against them.
But the man is just waving his hand.
"There's one saying it, the other says," he murmurs.
Ostravsko becomes a large laboratory of deregulation.
After the New Year awaits the vast majority of apartments, which still have non-market rent in the Czech Republic.
Aktuálně.cz therefore decided to visit the "laboratory" in person.
Havířovská waiting room RPG is one of a total of four in the Ostrava region and on Tuesday at nine in the morning it is already full.
Immediately next to the door there is a security guard from the security agency and a starting device.
"Leader 2011", shines a special button on its touch screen.
One tap, and a paper with a number comes out of the machine.
You won't even be able to read "157" and your figure is already beeping on a large display under the ceiling of the waiting room.
In half a second is your turn.
An older married couple comes to ask about the rent after a while, but the rush in the waiting-match apparently does not concern the new leases at all.
Most people came to solve common problems as usual - requests for corrections, inefficiencies and the like.
Letters with the new lease agreements have been received by the tenants most out of a total of 4,000 RPG portfolio apartments since about mid-November since mid-November.
RPG Byty prominns in their letters proposing an increase of the rent by 15 to 38 percent.
There was a special counter for new rents at the client centre on November 18.
"It's running for over a week, so it's only in the run-in," the workers behind the counter after a moment's hesitation, when the client introduces himself as a journalist.
De-regulation and Bakala's former mining flats are a populous theme.
Before the Election, the vice-chairman of the parliament and Ostrava native Lubomír Zaorálek of the ČSSD took it quite loudly, and even now the local newspapers are full.
"Twenty-five years I've wanted a new floor."
"They didn't even change my plate in the toilet," the mentioned man relieved at the beginning.
So far, according to the workers, pensioners come to see if they will get a discount on the lease if they sign on time, as the RPG promises them in a special offer.
A mid-super-in-sum discount on the February rent will also be given to other tenants who will sign the new contract by December 15.
Havířov "deregulatory" septum is definitely not overwhelmed with queries.
"From yesterday's 360-year-old clients there were only about forty-fifteen to about fifty people because of the rent for 2011," the worker said.
At the same time, the RPG proposes the individual negotiation of treaties on the web and its bulletin boards where it will be necessary.
However, if both parties do not arreach the lease, it will have to be determined by an independent court.
"This isn't about threatening, it's just information about possible and completely lawful legal action," the RPG said.
"No one will sued here," thinks one of the workers of RPG Byty and the man on the street in front of the client center partially agrees with her.
"I've don't have a 13-thousand-dollar retire and I'm left with five grand to eat, I don't have what it takes to sue," he said.
After that, the worker is insecure.
"We'll see."
"Mr. Zaorálek tells them in the media not to sign, so people are probably waiting," he said.
The regulation of rents ends in the majority of the Czech Republic with New Year's Day 2011 and, according to RPG Byty, their increase is an economic necessity.
"Existing rents still do not reach the amount necessary for the simple maintenance of a housing stock," recalls Pavel Klimeš, director of a residential portfolio for RPG Byty, that the angry tenant-repropriated negotiability of the housing stock is, according to homeowners, the main reason for the increase in rents.
Whereas in five years between 2005 and 2010, RPGs could have invested $4 billion in flats, in four years from 2011 to 2014, it is expected to be 5.2 billion.
The large-area screen hanging by the ceiling opposite the padded seats of the Havířov waiting room is repeating a few inferiorly highlighted information.
"Lease rent increased in 2011 by 15% and less affects 31 200 apartments, that's more than a 90% of RPG flats."
"And it's always going to be 7.50 CZK per square metre and a month," it will appear on the screen.
Only for 400 apartments from the RPG portfolio, the rent will rise by 38 percent.
"In these cases, it is an increase from an extremely low amount of 19.66 crowns per square meter," warns the RPG.
For 2600 apartments, the rent will remain the same next year.
RPG Byty promises that deregulation won't expel leases, but will be spread over four- to eight years.
In addition, it adds an additional commitment: all the money from the rent increase (see chart) the company puts it back into the apartments, mainly into thermal savings - new windows and insulating.
Primarily, they should be given to people who will sign an agreement to increase rents.
The offices also include a nursery, the architects understood
The AVriopoint administrative building in Brno is the new seat of Arch.Design.
No one would have anticipated that it also hid mini-kindergarten with everything that goes with it - with colorful walls, imaginative furniture, a bunch of toys and even a miniature playground on an outdoor patio.
Since October of last year it can be attended by the children of the employees of Arch.Design.
The projection-architectural company Arch.Design, from whose faculties a number a number of important Brno buildings emerged, counted on space for its corporate mini-kinders since the first drawings of the Avriopoint building.
It employs almost 90 people, mostly at the age of a babyboom, and it cared that women designers and architects could quickly get back to work.
Together with the possibility of part-time work, the nursery is a benefit that Arch.Design offers to its employees as part of a social program.
Builders and architects thus have the opportunity to reconcile a demanding job with caring for a child.
The mini-kindergarten was devised by the employees of Arch.Design and, with the support of management, they managed to obtain a subsidy of the European Union and the Ministry of Labour and Social Affairs.
The corporate mini-kindergarten is financed from the funds of the European Social Fund through the Operational Programme Human Resources and Employment and the state budget of the Czech Republic.
Thanks to grant funding (subsidies in the amount of CZK 2.8 million), they were able to purchase facilities and equipment and provide qualified personnel and operations for a period of two years.
After the financial subsidy is exhausted (2 years), Arch.Design is obliged to operate a mini-kindergarten at its own expense for a period of 3 years.
The preparation of the project lasted for one year and Economy Rating was significantly assisted in applying for the subsidy.
Care is provided by qualified staff, children are cared for every day by a nurse and a kindergarten teacher, so children can go to kindergarten without an age limit, yet the majority are over 3 years of age.
Since it is a "mini-kindergarten", at the same time only 5 children can be present at the same time, but during the day there will also be more.
The operation of the nursery is flexible according to the needs of the parents, and the children of all employees have the same right to use the nursery, regardless of the hierarchy status in the company.
Although it is an individual upbringing and care, the program content in the mini-kindergarten is comparable to the framework program of pre-school education.
Babies not only play here, but they are also provided with high-quality tuition - from basic cocquaintances to preparation for school desks, the English language is also taught.
The class is equipped not only with toys, stimulating games, but also a computer that children can take advantage of.
The presence of young children directly in the company creates a friendly and positive environment.
Parents can check their child at any time and the stressful situations are eliminated with delayed pick-off of children from nurseries away from their parents' workplace.
Parents thus use working hours more productively.
It is no wonder that there is a naturally loyal employee-employer-employment relationship.
Arch.Design thus ranks among Czech companies, which offer a significant benefit in recruiting new employees, are family-friendly and through this program has strengthened personnel marketing.
The house of bones fascinates even after 100 years. Take a look into the interiors from Gaudí
The roof looks like the spine of a primordial lizard, the staircase is reminiscent of a dinosaur's backbone and the balconies are an open fish mouth.
Such is the house Casa Batlló in Barcelona by the Catalan architect Antoni Gaudí.
Thanks to its organic shapes, the house has been nicjublonially nicknamed the House of Bones.
Organic shapes, savage colors, unusual use of stone, fragments of tiles and mosaic, facade reminiscent of scales and ubiquitous soft curves, all this is typical of Antoni Gaudí secession.
The facade of the house is played by dream lines, tender colors and textures reminiscent of nature and animals.
At detailed observation you can see the geometricity, the rhythm, and a precisely premeditated order.
At night the whole facade of the houses sounds even more dramatic, it is illuminated in such a way that the impediment of gloss of colors is further enhanced.
Violation and the twisting of shapes with the same extent appears in the interiors.
Wood in the waves copies window frames, handrails, decorative elements of doors, peepholes and skies.
Batlló house, which stands in the Eixample district on Passeig de Grácia Street No. 43, was created by rebuild the 1877 house by the architect Emilio Sala Cortese.
Renovation took place for two years and the house was completed in 1906.
The investor, the wealthy industrialist Josep Batlló, originally insisted on demolishing an old building and building an entirely new one, but he soon embraced Gaudí's idea of an unusual and cheaper redevelopment.
An apartment house of Casa Batlló was not proposed by Antoni Gaudí himself.
It was co-operated by architect Josep Maria Jujol.
It was on his design board that decorations were created for the representative apartment of the owner of the real estate and the use of the mosaic on the façade.
A number of other creators and artists also worked on the façade.
Working together on the designs of Casa Batlló started the successful cooperation of Yujol with Gaudí.
Yujol participated in the construction of Gaudi's house Casa Milá i Güell Park, in which there are other Gaudí's works.
From the locals soon after its completion, the house earned the nickname "House of Bones" thanks to its organic shapes.
Others, due to a specific window opening mechanism, began to call it a "vouring house".
It is also said that at the height of the building there is the sword of St. George, Patroo of Catalonia.
Hidden religious motifs also contain other sculptural elements of the building.
Gaudí designed a unique system of central heating with ventilating vents and typical chimneys for his time.
Bizarre may seem their for forty-five-degree inclination.
In the creation of Gaudí absolutely disrespected the building regulations at that time and did not allow himself to be tied.
At the beginning of the construction it obtained a building permit from the city, but its data on the planned refurbished were so vague that two years later during the completion, the city council was horrified and asked for relievance.
Councillors were bothered, for example, by the fact that the construction also interfered with pillars in the public pavement.
Reparations have never taken place, on the contrary: only fifteen days after the city district's call, Josep Batlló filed (and has successfully obtained) permission to lease the two top floors of the building.
The proprietor himself moved to the lower floors.
In the gallery on the second floor there was a social salon and on the third floor of a bedroom and a private part of an apartment.
The ground floor was leased jewellery.
The whole six-storey house intersperses a staircase through which you can climb to the roof terrace itself.
Crucial elements of rebuilding were the abolition of all right angles and straight lines and replacing them with curved walls.
Gaudí also collaborated with the modern concept of interconnecting the premises.
The social part can be completely intertwined by opening a lounge door, and a larger company can comfortably communicate with each other without maintaining the privacy needed for more intimately conversations at the same time.
In addition to the new façade, the house with Gaudi’s rebuild has also acquired an unconventional roofing and two extra floors.
Imaginary is Gaudí's game with light, blue and white glazed tiles, which are clasped the inner courtyard, and skylights.
The game of two colors adds maximum light on all the floors of the house.
It was enough to simply bet on the principle that white reflects light more than dark, which, on the contrary, engulfs the sun's rays.
Casa Batlló house, which was included in the Unesco World Heritage Fund in 2005, can be visited by anyone.
On the first floor of the house there is the most spacious and opulent apartment accessible to the public.
It wasn't until 2002 that the owners opted for this step, to celebrate the cent 50th anniversary of the birth of the glorious make-up.
They stayed for the tours because they found out that it was a very advantageous business and, in addition, the tours were extended with common and attic spaces, including a roof terrace.
Entrance to the commemoration is definitely not negligible, amounts to 16.50 euros and students, retirees, groups and holders of tourist cards have a 20 percent discount.
The sum greatly exceeds the price of the entrance fee to other Gaudí buildings in the city.
If you want to taste the true atmosphere of the house with dragon turrets, you need to quickly vaccinate with one of the tenement residents and visit it right at home.
Choosing a mattress and grater: women have different requirements than men
It's not the ostentatious frame of the bed, but the quality of the lying surface, i.e. the mattress and grater, give the basis of a really good bed.
However, it must harmoniously be complemented in order to suit a specific user.
When selecting, a lot of things are determined, from weight, height, age, state of health as well as the gender of the owner.
It is also an important type of interior into which the mattress and grate are purchased.
Typical example?
For example, latex mattresses aren't suitable for weekend cottages where they don't regularly drown, because they "warm" more slowly.
In addition, they have a significantly higher weight than a standard polyurethane mattress.
At the same time, it is necessary to reverse them regularly.
If they don't have a lot of venting bloating bloats in the nucleus, and they still lie on a solid plate, they retain moisture: Mold could also appear under them.
The custom also determines the choice.
Those who have spent a longer period of time abroad, where very high mattresses with a softening layer are often used, it will hardly feel good on a low and with a hard surface.
"Perhaps all the hockey players who came back from the NHL complained to me that they weren't going to sleep here."
"For their battered shoulders, heels and hips they needed mattresses with a softening layer of lazy foam."
"Suches are also recommended even for people with diabetes who have a nervous system disorder, so they misremove "information" from joints and skin," explains Sylva Hadamczik, who specializes in upholstered furniture.
Therefore, many hockey players, as well as other customers, were looking for mattresses with lazy foam years ago years ago.
"Today it's fine, a suitable mattress has Tempur, Italian Magniflex, but also the Czech Tropico."
You don't have to be an NHL player to appreciate the lazy foam.
This "miracle" works simply.
Optimizes its stiffness and shape according to the absorbed heat, or it adapts to your warm body.
There's nothing pushing you, you won't rewind at night like after a tough game.
You just get a better sleep.
Poar foam also acts as a protection against bedsores.
When choosing the size of the mattress to its height, add the length of the foot, which tends to be about 21 to 34 cm.
A man and a woman with a height of about 180 cm need a bed of 210 to 220 cm.
The minimum width for a single should be for a double bed of 90 cm, i.e. a total of 180 cm.
For separate beds, such as singlists, it is recommended at least 120 to 140 cm.
For the comfort and a possible visit.
"Otherwise, the rule for the width of the bed is simple: when lying on the back, you should have your hands comfortably slightly shrugged at the elbow," advises Ing. Helena Prokopová, guimer of the Guild of upholsterers and decorators, who has been practicing the issue of healthy sleeping and ergotomy of furniture for a number of years and lecturing it at vocational schools.
For a double bed everyone needs its own grater and a mattress, but here be careful when buying to match the heights of the mattresses.
Otherwise, you will have no choice but to mount grates to different heights, but you may not be able to do that with each bed frame.
Foam mattresses should have a height of at least 14 centimeters, types with a spring skeleton of at least 15, of which at least three centimeters count on a shaping and baking layer.
"When touching your hands or sitting on the mattress, you definitely can't feel the springs," warns Helena Prokopová.
Each mattress requires a certain type of grater.
Who upholstered beds, when the mattresses and the substrate are like merged Siamese twins, have nothing to do in the bedrooms.
Mattresses do not ventilate badly, it is not possible to wash their cover, which is already common today.
And because in the night we usually sweat 0.2 to 0.75 liters of sweat, it is clear that after five to seven years the mattress begins to resemble one large (and clogged) sieve.
That’s why experts reject advertising slogans about maternity-of-thresties for up to 25 years, or even lifetime guarantees.
However, everything has its own.
That’s why practical advice from retailers: do not give up a remembrance for mattresses with a width of 120 or 140 cm hygiene and a removable couster, but add a mattress protector, which you can lightly remove and wash.
Even though mattress covers are routinely produced today, so after unzipping we get two parts (for washing and the size of the washing machine very important!), however, manipulation on larger types (and in one person) when sensuing could resemble a light-heavyweight duel with a heavy one.
Do you have a solid slab in bed instead of a grater?
Never!
Maximum bed designed for one-night stands.
However, you can still provide the sachet (if it already doesn't have them) ventilation openings, using a mattress with a spring-boot and a height of at least 15 cm with springs in sachets (taschenfederkren) and a bouldering layer.
Polyurethane mattresses do not belong on such a substrate.
For non-allocation slat grates with 14 to 18 slats will be suitable latex or sandwich mattresses (such as a combination of latex and coconut), but you will always need grates with a larger number of slats, 28 or more, where the slats are duplicated in the lumbar area and their stiffness can be regulated.
The reclining lamellar grates do do not have mattresses with a spring skeleton, because they do not respond to positioning.
In addition to adjust the rigidity of slats, you can also indulge the so-called shoulder cradle.
It is a special attachment of slats, which makes it possible to push or even tilt the lamella in such a way so that the shoulder of the sleeper nicely "shaps".
Definitely an important thing for men who "shoulder across their backs".
It is even remembered by the manufacturers of mattresses, who began to use the softening zone just in the area of the shoulders.
They also remember women - they indulge in a softer surface in places of the hip.
For the most demanding customers there will be wing or plate grates with so-called multi-torn suspension.
The pressure spreads to multiple points and the voltage decreases.
However, beware, as soon as you apply for the correct mattress and grater the wrong pillow (large and "blown"), most "plus" will disappear.
The basic rule is simple: the pillow is supposed to support the head and the toes only extend to the shoulders so that they can relax the shoulder muscles.
As a suitable size, it is recommended 50 x 60 cm, which they prefer, for example, in Scandinavia.
When lying on the side, the pillow should straighten (together with the shoulder cradle grater or a softened zone in the mattress).
After all, modern mattresses have something to do with the original strawberries stuff-filled grasses.
Increasingly, their manufacturers use natural materials or at least an adjectives.
It’s also a trend in bio mattresses.
Raw materials from petroleum from which polyurethane musculoskes are replaced by natural oils, such as jumping and soybeans.
Soybean is used by all well-familiar manufacturers.
"Bio in mattresses means that the kernel is made of material of purely natural origin," explains Petr Vasicek from Tropico Plus.
Natural substances are also used in the production of mattress coverings, one of the first was aloe vera.
But you can also indulge in microcapsules to relax the fresh scent of the forest or lavender.
There is also a division for men and women.
While men "cooling" plant birsh plant extract in order for the mattress coating to absorb heat and cool the body, on the contrary, women's "drunk covers".
Red pepper extract increases the circulation of the blood, so the weaker sex is warmer.
Today's favorites?
Mattress made of cold foam (it has larger pores with a broken cellular structure, is thus more breathable than conventional polyurethane and also offers better elasticity.
A combination of various rigidities of cold foams and "lazy" foam creates multi-zone mattresses, which knows exactly where the head, loins and heels should be.
For a high-quality mattress you will pay from seven thousand crowns and above.
Those that meet the health parameters have a lower VAT rate, are taken as medical aids.
Gumotex was the first to achieve this advantage in the Czech Republic.
Saving a mattress and grate isn't easy.
Sometimes it is possible to take advantage of events of manufacturers or sellers, who offer two mattresses for the price of one.
Magniflex, for example, offers Duofreeze mattress with sewn-colored memory mole memory foam to cover for 11 990 crowns.
However, the latter can already be purchased free of charge.
The era of lazy or viscoelastic foams with an open cellular structure and a high-density kick-started "cosmalic" Temprus material at a "cosmic" pace.
It originated originally as a material for NASA, because astronauts were subjected to enormous congestion during take-off.
The pace in the heat softens, on the contrary, it is strengthened in the cold.
It perfectly adapts to the warm human body and distributes its pressure optimally.
Therefore, it is ideal against the appearance of bedsores, but also for increasing sleep comfort.
Viscoelastic foams are used not only for mattresses, but also for medical products, such as special pillows under the feet, which lighten the legs and venous system.
Domestic manufacturers, for example, use Eucatherm C or Twinsaver mousse and the like.
More and more parents want to monitor people, so they want to guard their children
Worried about your own child brings more and more people to the idea of self-suading via GPS.
The system originally created for searching for cars can also monitor the movement of people and thus help, for example, in cases such as the disappearance of nine-year-old Anička Janatková.
So far, the device is about as big as a matchbox, but it can soon only be as big as a watch.
Many people were terrified by the case of the missing Annie and began to look for the means to better protect their children.
Even at the cost of intrusing their privacy.
"We are trying to tell everyone that it is not a spy, but an additional means to reduce the likelihood that a tragedy will happen," says Petr Pilin from Macro Weil, which is one of the suppliers of surveillance equipment to the Czech market.
"I'm admitting that in a certain case there may be an abuse of such devices, for example, when they put in a bag to someone who doesn't know about it but haven't encountered such a case yet."
"Most of the time, customers actually buy it for safety reasons," Pilin added.
According to him, the child should always know for what purpose the "box" serves and how it is used in case of an emergency.
The child psychologist Dana Bočková points out that if the child does not want to wear anything like this, he will simply get rid of it.
"Even very young children show a lot of creativity in how to intentionally lose some things when they don't want to be under control."
"Various anticipation has previously been triggered by mobile phones, which, however, can be switched off in the same way as these tracking devices," Bočková said.
According to her, it is also questionable whether in the event of an inflicting such device would actually help.
"With regard to the inaugural kidnapping, the perpetrator will practically always ensure that the child does not have any electronics that could divulge his position."
"Now it's the phone."
"When the tracking devices begin to expand, they will also be looking for them, because their sale will certainly be supported by massive advertising."
"In such a case, criminals will always be one step ahead of the police," the psychologist says.
However, it admits that a similar device can help in the event of an unnecessary pedophile infestation.
According to Robert Cech from the company RC Monitoring, which offers surveillance services both for cars and for persons, even in the event of immediate destruction of the surveillance equipment, its presence can help.
"Equipment can be set for regular location after a specified period of time."
"Breasurements of it to be harmed, at least the last position of the person concerned will be available."
"Exceeding devices have built-in tiltometers, so even if a person just slips and stays immovably lying down, the device immediately sends a warning with the indication of the position," Čech said.
According to him, a similar service can be handled by smart mobile phones with navigation, where you just need to upload even special software.
The problem of the current surveillance units lies in the fact that they are either devices similar to pendants the size of a syrup box or smaller mobile phones.
There is always a risk of their loss.
According to Pilina from Macro Weil, a device that looks like a regular digital watch is now being tested.
"That's going to have the biggest advantage in that any aggressor doesn't have to know the tracking device at all."
"Or he only recognizes them after a while, and it can give the police an extra time," he adds.
Whether the tracking devices will be put into bracelets or pendants, they will still use the same technology that is used to guard the cars.
It is a combination of position detection via the GPS system (which use common autonavigations) and the function of the mobile phone, which reports the current position of the car.
