To this day I draw from the contacts I have gained thanks to the competition
Lukas Pytloun helped Lukáš Pytlou in selling a franchise of his hotel.
Lukáš Pythuloun, a twenty-eight-year-old businesswoman from Liberec, became the winner of last year’s Tradesman of the Year competition.
His story of success is like from a movie.
Lukas Pytloun liked to travel.
And abroad, he realized that there is a lack of high-quality accommodation at a sensible price in Liberec.
From savings from foreign brigades and using a bank loan, he bought a semi-detached house with five flats.
For the fact that he was still a third-year college student at the time, it was of great courage.
"He didn't give me the ratio between the quality and the price of accommodation from us so I wouldn't try to do something about it," says Lukáš Pytloun.
The first refurbished apartment began to be leased to foreign workers.
He invested all the profits back into the house, which he gradually converted into a guesthouse with 54 beds.
Since then, in seven years of doing business, it has gradually opened four guesthouses, two stylish restaurants and two hotels with a total capacity of more than 300 beds of all categories of accommodation.
The most comfortable of them until recently was the four-nast Pytloun design hotel.
This year, however, he has added another, equally high-quality.
"Right we have signed a contract with a hotel in Ostrov u Tisá, where we have sold our franchizing concept, and other people interested in Pytloun franchise are reporting," says last year's number one tradesman.
"And on this sale of the license to operate the hotel, it was the victory in the Trade Licensor of the Year competition," Pytloun acknowletently added.
Winning brought him greater affiliation and popularity in Liberec and its surroundings.
"The people got to know me on the streets, they often congratulatently congratulatent me and I have to say that it was pleasant," he recalled.
Participating in the competition definitely recommends to everyone who is strived for something.
It is not even necessary to win, every visibility is good.
He says that to this day he draws from contacts and acquaintances, to which he might not have gotten in any other way.
"I've started trading regularly with some of whom I've encountered thanks to the competition."
Glory associated with the success, according to him, fell a bit on every employee as well.
And he's got about seventy of them.
"I observe greater loyalty and pride in being involved in something that has gained universal recognition."
"It is far from just my personal achievement, it's the result of a good job for all of us together."
"It's just my name to the forefront," Lukáš Pytloun says modestly.
I wish my successor a quieter year than we had in Farmet
Euphoria from the victory was quickly replaced by the effects of the crisis.
Thanks to the purposefulness of the victor, Farmet has had worse times.
Karel Žďárský, the owner of Farmet, arrived for the prize for the first place in the previous Vodafone Company of the Year competition in Slovanský dům last year.
At that time, the jury appreciated the efforts through which he built a company with hundred-million-dollar turnover from a small tool shop in Česká Skalice.
"I can say that the award encouraged not only me, but all my collaborators and increased our self-confid/confidence for the match with the competition," the winner said.
At the outset of Farmet's history was the desire to do something useful.
Thus, when the state offered his father Karel Žďárský to rent a small workshop as compensation for communist-seated property, he did not hesitate for a long time, he refused a career in the company where he was then working, and with a couple of friends around his father they founded a company for plastic bathtubs.
Soon after, they began to specialize in the presses of vegetable oil.
After eighteen years it became the largest manufacturer of automatic presses in Central Europe.
A greater awareness even among non-professional public has gained the company after the achieved success in the competition through the media.
"Not only our customers began to perceive the Farmet brand significantly better, but also foreign partners began to appreciate that they cooperate with a company that has achieved greater success in their country," says Karel Žďárský.
The past year, however, it has not only been a sign of euphoria from victories.
Valuation was based on the company's financial results until 2008 and only partially from 2009.
2009 was the hardest year for the sale of engineering goods.
Crisis culminated, which did not yet affect citizens so much, but companies were already halting their investment projects and began limiting purchases of new machines and technologies.
"Of course, this situation hasn't even avoided us."
"Storage decreased by 40 percent, we had to limit production and there were layoffs," explains Karel Žďárský.
A viable company sometimes has to be able to take such unpopular measures.
However, they managed the situation and kept the company profitable, albettle smaller than before the crisis.
However, from what they did not compromise in Farmet is the investment in research and development.
"From the subsidies we built a research and training center, we strengthened the presentation at the international trade fairs, won the main awards in domestic exhibitions," says Žďárský.
It all suggests that the crisis lies behind them.
Recurregnancies are rising again, and with them the number of new employees.
"I wish the new victor a pleasant feeling about the victory, but in a calmer atmosphere," says Karel Žďárský.
Poll that removes nonsensical regulations
A poll about Absurdity of the Year is part of a business competition for the fourth time.
Some nonsensical regulations have already been abolished.
An inglorious vacant winner of the next edition of the Absurdity of the Year poll became the obligation to wear documents to the office, which are also accessible to officials online.
This was decided by more than one-three of the less than four-thousand-voters.
For example, the social security administration required a paper extract from the Commercial Register, even if an official can obtain it with two clicks of the mouse.
Thanks to the poll, it will change.
"The Czech Social Security Administration will no longer require an extract from the Commercial Register during inspections of insurance payers," CSSA spokeswoman Jana Buraňová said.
Enshrining on all the regulations and regulations that companies and entrepreneurs in the Czech Republic have to comply with, and the nonsensical ones to abolish is the meaning of the Absurdity of the Year competition.
This year it is a fourth part of a business competition organized by Hospodářské noviny and is becoming more and more and more and more popular among the public.
If about 3,000 voters took part in the vote last year, this year there have been almost four-thousand.
