Two L-159 jets for the first time in foreign hands in Spain
Two L-159 aircraft out of a total of five, for which the Czech Ministry of Defense replaced the Casa C-295M transport aircraft, on Tuesday flew to Sevilly, Spain, which is headquartered by the aircraft manufacturer Casa - EADS.
This is the first time that machines of this type have emerged in the possession of someone other than the Czech army.
Abroad so far they have only been on exercises or demonstration years.
These are two-seater L-159T1, which have so far been operated by the Czech army and which have been airworthiest.
The remaining three machines are a single-seater L-159.
In the meantime, they stand preserved in Aero Vodohochody and shop intermediary - Omnipol - took them over last the past week.
According to Jan Pejšek from the Defense Press Department, when the aircraft will physically depart from Vodochod is not yet known. According to Jan Pejšek.
"One-digit L-159-shift aircraft have been earmarked from long-term-secured aircraft, without further deposits and investments on the part of the ministry's department."
"Two-seatted L-159T1 was exchanged in its current condition and form," Pejšek told Právo.
He added that the army will not stay without two-seater jets, because in May 2009 the ministry signed a contract with Aero Vodochody worth CZK 200 million to rebuild four unneeded L-159 machines into two two-seater machines.
One new aircraft was taken over by the army in July, the other will be taken over by the end of this year.
According to Pejsek, EADS has pledged to push through some alliance countries into armament.
"It is clearly stated in the contract that the buyer of the L-159 aircraft is EADS and they will be used to train pilots from NATO countries."
"It is therefore a matter for the customer, how it will implement this matter," Pejšek said.
Hospodářské noviny recently stated that, for example, the Spanish army was not interested in the L-159.
Excurrition of five L-159 for one Casa aircraft and the purchase of another three aircraft for 3.5 billion crowns was approved by the government of Mirek Topolánek last year.
The Ministry of Defence did not announce a tender for the purchase and approached EADS directly.
At that time, the defense leadership argued that the exchange would support future L-159 exports abroad.
The Czech army has bought 72 machines from Aera and uses only 24, the rest would like to sell.
Purchasing Casa planes is likely to have a trial.
The European Commission has sued the Czech Ministry of Defense in a European court because it was supposed to select aircraft on the basis of a pan-European tender and not by addressing one manufacturer.
The army has already taken over three Casa aircraft, the last should arrive at Prague's Kbely airport within a fortnight.
Longer forefinger than the ring finger indicates a lower risk of cancer
Men who have an inefnocreat longer than a ring finger are at a lower risk of getting prostate cancer.
That's the surprising conclusion of a study published by the British Journal of Cancer on Wednesday.
It can be related to the level of the male hormone testosterone.
"Our results show that the relative length of the finger can serve as a simple prostate cancer risk test, especially for men under the age of 60 years old," study co-author Rosa Eeles, a professor at the Institute of Cancer Research at the University of Warwick said in a statement.
The finger could thus help determine which men should undergo a test for prostate cancer, especially in combination with a genetic risk or other threatening factors, such as a family health burden, Eeles added.
Between 1994-2009 she and her colleagues in Britain tracked more than 1,500 pro-Castropoiquican cancer patients, concomitantly more than 3,000-super-swomen.
More than half of the study participants had their index finger on the right hand shorter than a ring finger.
It was the same probability of prostate cancer as 19 percent of those whose toes were just as long.
However, in the case of those who had an index finger longer than the ring, this risk fell by 33 per cent, for men up to the age-up to the age of 87 per cent.
The ratio of longitudinal fingers - formed in the prenatal period - apparently reflects the different levels of the sex hormones to which the baby is exposed in the uterus.
Fewer Testoster Testosterone Responsibles.
Earlier research has shown testosterone that promotes growing prostate cancer.
Professor Václav Pačes told the Czech News Agency that he knows this study curfew, but it does not appear to be "completely proven" by the relationship between the length of his fingers and cancer.
