These are some of the results of an up-to-to-date Latinorómetro poll, which took place in 18 countries and was published exclusively in The Economist.
As reconnaissance has taken place regularly since 1995, it can monitor changes in attitudes across the region.
In this year's poll, which took place in September and at the beginning of October, two related things are dominated.
The first is that Latin America is in a relatively cheerful mood.
The second is an increase in the stability of stances towards democracy and its key institutions.
Promoting democracies has risen significantly in several countries on the periphery in South America (see Table 1).
For example, in Peru, where economic growth has an average of 6% per annum since 2002, democracy support has risen from a low of 40% in 2005 to 61% this year.
It has also risen in Mexico, where economics was recovering after a deep drop in production last year.
But only about half of those surveyed in Mexico and Brazil, two most populous areas, are convinced democrats, which draws the regional average (see Graph 2).
About 44% of respondents across the entire area expressed satisfaction with how democracy works in practice in their country, it is the same number as last year, but compared to 2001, when it was 25%, it is a big increase (graph 3).
Roughly 34% said they trusted the assembly in their country and 45% trust the government, once again the same numbers as last year, but it is a big difference compared to 2003 (17% and 24%).
Ten years ago, democracy in Latin America was undermined by an economic crunch.
Support arose during the global financial crisis probably due to the fact that many Latin American countries suffered only a short recession and enjoyed a strong revival.
In addition, a stronger social security network has helped protect poor people.
(The United Nations ECONOMIC Commission for Latin America announced this week that the ratio of Latino-waiters living in Poverty had risen only slumularly in 2009 and this year has fallen to 32% again.)
However, the mood verse varies in a wide range from the ground to the ground (see Graph 4).
Brazil's strong economic performance and the popularity of its outnumbered President Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva are reflected in a widely accepted sense of progress.
This situation is rarer in Mexico and Central America, where the recess was more serious and the state of forcible crime deteriorated has deteriorated.
For only the second time when surveys began to be carried out, more respondents across Latin America have seen a crime rather than anemployment (see Graph 5).
Roughly 31% say that either they themselves or their close relatives became victims of crime last year, but this is a decrease compared to the figure of 38% from last year and is also the lowest number since 1995.
More massive economic performance is reflected in increasing support for private entrepreneurship and market-efficiencies: around 71% of respondents across the area say that private enterprise is essential for development, this percentage has grown from 56% in 2007 (see Graph 6).
About 58% agree that the market economy is the only system that will allow the development of their country, this percentage has risen from 47% from last year.
Interestingly, 62% of respondents in Venezuela have the same opinion (and 80% support private business there), although their left-wing President Hugo Chávez expropriates many private firms.
Brazil is a country that has a leading position in the area, according to 19% of respondents (compared to 18% last year), followed by the United States (9%, unreplacing compared to last year) and Venezuela (9%, compared to 11% last year)
Nevertheless, the United States is considered the most influential country by responders in Mexico and in large parts of Central America, with Venezuela enjoying success in Ecuador, the Dominican Republic and Nicaragua.
Criticized Elections
No one did not expect the General Elections in Haiti on November 28.
However, they were much worse than outsiders trying to help the affected country to be afraid.
Elections marked filling urns and recurring votes, ballots scattered on canals, shattered polling stations and intimidation.
Electoral polling stations open after an hour's delay.
Numerous Haitians, including many relocated due to the January quarreling, roamed around trying to figure out where they could vote.
In the end, some of them gave up.
More than a dozen presidential candidates, including several anticipated outliners, shouted that it was a hoax and they were calling for the abolition of the election.
Protests, some of them of a forcible character, were spreading all over the country.
Yet Edmond Mulet, a high-profile U.N. official in Haiti, declared that "it was a pretty good election in many respects."
Observers from the U.S United States Organization and the Karib Community cautiously said that "doesn't believe these inconsistenciencies, no matter how serious they may not necessarily repeal the process."
In other words, the show continues.
The electoral body whose opposition votes are bound by René Préval, an unpopular outgoing president and his nominee Jude Célesin, is set to announce preliminary results on 7 December.
Explained decisive duel is scheduled for January.
All electoral hopes aren't lost.
The two leading opposition candidates Michel Martella, a rapper and Mirlande Manigat, a former first-aid, withdrew from their original call for a vacancy.
However, the problem may arise if Mr Célestine, who has little or negligible support among the electoral electorate, will be identified by the electoral commission for achieving a decisive duel.
Due to a rampaging cholera outbreak and more than 1 million people who are still living in tent tents after the quarreling, there were fears that voter turnout would be low.
In the end, many Haitians wanted to vote, but due to disorganization they were prevented.
Rough Roughly 250,000 identity cards that are necessary for voting remained undivided shortly before the e-election.
There's a lot at stake.
The donors see the election of a legitimate and popular government as a crucial step in reconstructing Haiti.
Therefore, it is clear that the Haitians will do it on their own.
After peaks in the center
Cristina Fernández, an Argentinian’s president, is benefiting from public sympathies where her popularity has strengthened from $20 to more than a 50%, the highest level since she was elected in 2007.
Nevertheless, the reversement of this success into a successful second-perieficial campaign in next year's elections may depend on whether grieving Lady Fernández renounces her deceased husband, precursor and political partner Néstor Kirchner.
And on these characters, everything will be founded.
Mr Kirchner, who continued economic policy even after his wife successfully became president, had a habit of compulsoring budgets elevating tax revenues, economic growth, and inflation.
This allowed the first couple to spend additional income at will.
Fernández's government lost a majority in Congress last year.
But the Opposition split and was further destabilised at Mr Kirchner's death on a heart attack on October 27.
