But after chaotic sessions (who were slapping and accusation of corruption), Congress failed to approve the 2011 budget.
"I'm not arguing," the president said.
The budget for 2010 will now also be in place for next year, with the president having more space to spend at will.
The economy is overgroaccuminarily overgroamount: growth of up to 9% is likely this year.
The official rate of inflation now stands at 11 percent, but many private estimates and trade unionists are talking about a figure that is approaching 30%.
Mr Kirchner, who went into office in 2003 when Argentina was plagued by an economic collaboration, set a priority on growth and poverty reduction, rather than controlling inflation.
He declined to act with the IMF, burdened the private sector and purged INDEC, a statistical agency that has acceded to monitor official inflation statistics.
Can President Fernández now moderate this policy?
She called for talks between private businesses and trade union leaders.
And its officials say they want to limit wage growth to 18% next year.
The economy was supported by the rise in global prices for the export of Argentine agricultural products.
But inflation was disrupted by growth based on a large devaluation in 2002.
This may be the reason why President Fernández lets us know that she wants to rebuild Argentine financial ties to the rest of the world.
Earlier this year, the government created an agreement with bondholders who previously rejected a debt restructuring that Argentina did not pay in 2001.
President Fernández is now facing an agreement with the creditor’s governments, which are grouped at the Paris Club, which she owes about $6.7 billion.
Kirchners has always insisted that this fact should not be within sight of the IMF, as is customary.
But last month Amado Boudou, the economy minister announced that the fund would provide "technical assistance" in order to propose a new national consumer-price index to commemorating an introduction to INDEC purification.
It is still unclear whether President Fernández will want to stay in politics after the end of her term when her husband is no longer at her side.
If it wants to stay, he needs to get the support of the middle-grade, which is tired by Kirchnerism.
At the time the Argentinians' return from their summer vacation, which they have in March, a wave of sympathies will decline.
If she wants to win the next election, she will have to decide whether it will not be necessary to quietly renounce the many of the opinions her husband held in politics.
Half annoyance for China
Taiwanese electrifiers are closer to closer connections to the mainland.
What does Taiwan think about her big brother across the sea?
More than 7.5 million voters, 70% of the beneficiaries, voted mayors and city councils in five borough districts on 27 November, which can be considered a broader assessment of President Ma Ying-jeou.
President Ma himself is a representative of the feelings that predominate on the mainland and have a strong commitment in business relations, including an agreement on partially freed trade, in the greatest settlement of relations with China since 1949.
President Ma went through a retreat, but only narrowly.
Although his Kuomintang (KMT) party ended up winning three of the mayor's five offices, it could not have been able to win by a majority of votes.
In addition, the Democratic Progressive Party (DPP), the Democratic Progressive Party (DPP), which is tielzing Taiwan's greater independence, has won nearly 50% of all votes against the KMT 45%.
If it was a presidential election, President Ma would have trouble.
Since it was elected two years ago, the share of his party's vote has been reduced by about 8%.
For the first time, the DPP party, together with KMT, was on city councils.
Elections took place, like most Taiwanese elections, which include noisy electoral trucks and mass rallies with flair-sponsors with sparse-clad girls.
It even included a compulsory mysterious assassination attempt.
Sean Lien, the son of Lien Chan, the honorary chairman of KMT, was an agitated for KMT candidates on the e-election.
A man from the mob shot the young Mr. Lien over his face, injure him and killed the spectator.
Bizarrely it was not determined whether the militant intended to injure Mr. Lien or settling a dispute with the nominee.
In any case, Tsai Ing-wen, the DPP leader, argues that the shooting affected the election, suspicion falls on the DPP and leads the electorate towards KMT.
Probably the only big winner of the campaign was China.
Removable how the question of the mainland itself remained in the background.
Instead, nominees have struggled over plans for state housing construction and job-creation.
Taiwan usually enjoys its de facto independence and hates China's military threats.
KMT therefore tends to escalate ties to the government in Beijing.
But this time the DPP also refrained from waging anti-Chinese stances.
Responsibility to reality and the realisation that Chinese commerce is crucial for Taiwan's economic survival.
On behalf of his party, President Ma confirmed that China's policies are acceptable to the majority of Taiwan.
The government in Beijing can be expected to continue commercial bribery, together with its strategy to lure Taiwan back into the her flock.
China did not spend time sending a negotiator to Taipeye to set up a summit on the protection of investment and medical cooperation.
I'm sure they'd also like to see political interdeprecation, but in order.
It would be better to await it after the 2012 presidential election.
In any case, the DPP party discovered itself.
Two years ago she was at the wrong level: her former leader, Chen Shui-bian, was sent to prison on corruption charges.
The president in 2000-08 was a sebacest and a proponent of clear independence.
A new generation has evolved under the leadership of Mrs. Tsai, which is moderate and the DPP is working hard to attract experts and young people.
Mrs Tsai was prosecuted as a DPP candidate for president, such as Su Tseng-chchange, who held office under Mr. Chen.
Their party is now doing its best to show that they can also work with Beijing.
Mrs Tsai has published plans for research institutes, which serve as a contact point between DPP-China, assuming that their negotiations can take place without pre-regime.
Mrs Tsai informs the Taiwan public that her party represents a "credible alternative" to Mr Ma's hasty approach.
Mr. Chen appears to be angered by this shift.
In a statement released from his cell, he expressed the opinion that DPP candidates would do their best if they insisted that Taiwan was an independent country.
He even ordered Mr. Su, the pragmatist, not to run for the president.
But even without Mr. Chen's intervention, the DPP will fight for a selection among potential candidates.
KMT is eligible to find its own people.
Mr Ma's assessments were bad, despite the rapid pace of economic growth; KMT candidates did not want to go into the campaign with him.
Its unpopularity appears to be based mainly on unemployment and the deepening gap between the rich and the poor.
KMT spokeswoman says he will reorganise his Cabinet, as well as local branches.
Strongly promoted proposals to promote Taiwan to establish friendly contacts are restricted.
At the same time, the majority of Taiwan citizens consider economic integration unavoidable with China to be unavoidable.
According to Antonia Chiang, a prominent publicist, "the point from which there is no return has already passed."
