Grammy Awards nominations: Michael Daugherty, Steve Mackey lead in classical music category
Nominations for classical music have a wide range of musicians from all over the world at the 53rd Grammy Awards.
Michael Daugherty and his "Deus Ex Machina" for the piano and orchestra, with a number of five points and Steve Mackey with his "Dreamhouse", an eclectic orchestral work, with a number of three points.
Among the nominees ties in Southern California appeared Estonian composer Arvo Pärt and his Symphony #4 "Los Angeles", a work that was commissioned via Los Angeles Philharmonic and made its debut at the Walt Disney Concert Hall in 2009.
Composer based in L.A. Avner Dorman and his Mandolin Concerto also has a chant for an award.
Nominations in classical music are divided into 13 categories, which include orchestral, opera, choral and solo recordings.
The category for classical music producers of the year has five nominees, such as Blanton Alspaugh, David Frost, Tim Handley, Marina and Victor Ledin and James Mallinson.
Nominations for the classic album were won by Bruckner and its Symphony #3 and 4 from the Royal Concertgebouw Orchestra; Daugherty and his "Deus Ex Machina" and "Metropolis Symphony" by Nashville Symphony Orchestra; Steven Mackey and his "Dreamhouse" from the Boston Modern Orchestra Project and Synergy Vocals;
Regarding the opera category, the nominees are Berg and "Lulu" from Orchestra of the Royal Opera House; Adolf Hasse and "Marc Antonio e Cleopatra" by Ars Lyrica Houston; Kaija Saariaho and "L'Amour de Loin" by Deutsches Symphonie-Orchester Berlin; Rodion Shchedrin and "The Enchanted Wanderer" by Orchestra and Chorus.
Composers who are nominated for Grammy for contemporary compositions include Pärt for Symphony No. 4, Daugherty "Deus Ex Machina," Hans Werner Henze for "Appassionatee Plus," Magnus Lindberg for "Graffiti" and Shchedrin for "The Enchanted Wanderer."
Pärt's Symphony No. 4 premiered at the Disney Hall in January 2009 for a L.A. Philharmonic under the baton of Esa-Pekka Salonena.
Approximately a 30-minute piece is called "Los Angeles" and is dedicated to former oil tycoon Mikhail Khodorkovsky who is imprisoned in Russia.
Dorman and "Mandolin Concerto" were nominated for an instrumental performance by a soloist with an orchestra.
Composer from L.A. stated that he wrote a 15-minute work specifically for Mandolin performer Avi Avital, who performed at a concert with Metropolis Ensemble based in New York.
"I've been texting this with tones from the Middle East and many North African Rhythms," Dorman said.
Conducter Pierre Boulez, 85, received a nomination in the category for orchestral performance, for the leadership of the Chicago Symphony while recording Stravinsky's works.
Instrumental soloists who received the nomination include pianists Nelson Freire and Marc-André Hamelin; organist Paul Jacobs; violinist Julia Fischer; and harpist Sarah Schuster Ericsson.
Among the nominated vocal soloists this year were only women, Anne Sophie Von Otter, Cecilia Bartoli, Lucia Duchon, Vivica Genaux and Measha Brueggergosman.
Soprano Jessye Norman for her recording "Roots - My Life, My Song" was nomination.
