Arsenic-resistance microbeches increase the possibility of new types of life on Earth or elsewhere
The discovery of a strange bacterium that can use arsenic as one of its nutrients expands the space to search for new forms of life on Earth, and perhaps beyond.
Researchers have discovered an unusual bacterium here on the Earth, claiming that it is a sign that life has options that stand outside the main elements that have hitherto been considered and basic.
"This organism has a dual ability."
"It can grow either due to phosphorus or arsenic."
"This fact is very odd, but it is no proof that it is a form of truly 'extraterrestrial' life," Paul C. W. Davies of Arizona State University, collaborator of the report, which appeared on Thursday in an on-the-line journal Science.
For a long time it was considered necessary for life six main elements - carbon, hydrogen, nitrogen, oxygen, phosphorus and sulphuric.
However, the researchers found that the bacteria discovered in Mono Lake, California, are able to continue to grow after arsenic replaced by phosphorus.
"You'll start astonishing what's still possible," said Ariel D. Anbar of Arizona State University, collaborator.
This finding is significant for the search for life outside the Earth, as the researchers must be able to recognize life and know what life can look like, Anbar said.
The study diligides on Earth-based microbes.
However, a report on this news published to discuss it, which did not reveal the details of the finding, provoked widespread speculation on the Internet that the report reveals the discovery of extraterrestrial life.
That's not the case.
The discovery "distinguishes that other planetary organisms may be able to take advantage of other elements proving biochemistry, and that a set of elements that we deem 'standard' may not be so absolutely necessary for life," Charles Cockell, a professor at Planetary and Space Sciences Research Institute, the Open University in Milton Keynes.
Cockell wasn't part of the research team.
"This work is unpopular because it shows the replacement of a single element with an element of another in the basic biochemistry and biochemical structure," Cockell added.
It wasn't an accuchorous finding.
Felisa Wolfe-Simon of the NASA Astrobiology Institute, which led the study, focused on Mono Lake because it exhibits high arsion levels.
Arzen and phosphorus are chemically very similar, so she contemplated that the microbe, which is exposed to both elements, may be able to replace each other.
“Arzen is poisonous mainly because its chemical behavior is very similar to phosphorus.”
"As a result, it's very hard to detach these elements from each other."
"However, arsenic is so different that just as phosphorus does not work. When it gets into the body, it begins to work in biochemical processes," Anbar explained.
The researchers collect bacteria known as GFAJ-1 and expose them to an increasing arsenic concentration, which bacteria are capable of adapting and growing.
Microbes grow better under phosphorus, but since they can also survive an arsenic replacement, there is an increase in the possibility that the form of arsenic life can occur naturally, either elsewhere on Earth or on another planet or month where arsenic is more common.
Jamie S. Foster, a microbiology assistant professor at the University of Florida, stated that the idea of arsenic that could be replaced with phosphorus is not new, but there has never been an example on which this fact could be achieved.
Arzen was more common in earlier times on Earth, she said, so scientists speculated that it may have been exploited by earlier life forms.
"This suggests that there could have been other ways to create a life, not just the way it was created in the early period of the Earth," Foster, who was not part of a Wolfe-Simon research team.
Responsible by NASA, the Department of the Energy and the National Institute of Health
Remnant stolen dogs were found in Compton
The remaining two of the four show dogs that someone stole from the Bellflower motel were found. The sheriff of the Los Angeles region on Thursday.
