Khmmier Veterans are forced to cope with the past
In a diligently illuminated concrete classroom with soiled and peel-peeled walls, the headteacher of Anlong V-Veng High School reminded the man who built it, the Khmer Rouge leader Ta Mok.
"Everyone here loves this Mok."
"He was a good leader and he cared about his people," Sreng Kor Ma, 42 said.
Familier known as the "Butcher" due to its brutality during the reign of the Khmer Rouge, the commander remains popular in this distant Khmer Rouge possession in north-Western-West Cambodia, where he built hospitals, bridges and other infrastructure, and where thousands of former soldiers of this organization still live.
But this year, 12 years after the Khmer Khmer people renounced the government, long-lasting loyyalty is also undermined in Anlong Vengo.
In April, victims at the Truth and Reagreed Forum were allowed to publicly confront the people who were part of the regime.
In June, the government provided textbooks that for the first time were explaining the history of the Khmer Rouge and their former soldiers to the children.
And in July the United Nations and the Cambodian Judiciary, the first conviction of a former member of the Khmer Rouge, a whistle-agadoler, the notorious acquaintance Tuol Sleng, Kaing Khek Iev, better-known as the Spirit, to 35 years of impeiment.
With each such strevoked, there has been an increasing unerepency among Veterans of the Khmer Rouge in Anlong Veng, complicating efforts to reconcile and their attempts to re-engage in the Cambodian society.
"Among the former Khmer Khmeries is grievance and frustration, but they are helpless to do anything," Chhhang Youk, the head of the Kamboggi Documentation Center, an independent organization that examines the regime, said in a statement.
"For them, the life between the Khmer Rouge was wonderful, but the regime became a symbol of evil."
"It divides the families."
During the Khmer Rouge administration in Cambodia, from 1975 to 1979, an estimated 1.7 million people were executed or starvation, sick or overhauled.
When the Vietnamese invaded and overthrewed the Polish Pot government in 1979, remnants of the regime and its soldiers fled to the Cambodian border with Thailand.
There they launched an uprising, which lasted until the last movement in December 1998.
Pol Pot, Nuon Chea and Ieng Sary ran guerrilla warfare from their bases in the mountains and jungles of western Cambodia, while Ta Mok cultivated supporters in Anlong Vengu.
But in the mid-1990s, after the UN-sponsor-sponsor-sponsored peace agreement, the country was led to the first democratic elections in 1993. Large groups of Khmer Red fighters began to switch to a government culminated in Ieng Sara's surrender in 1996 and Nuon Chea and Khieu Samhan in 1998.
Pol Pot Died of Natural Breathing in 1998, and Ta Mok who persuaded in 1999 was captured by government forces in Dangrek.
He died in Phnom Penh prison in 2006 while awaiting a trial.
Their patrons no longer took care of them, the majority of the former Khmer Khmer fighters have sanctioned into an ordinary Cambodian rural life, and many of them nostalgically remember the time of the movement.
Although a few elite Khmer Khmes have retained their local government position in exchange for their weapons, their position remains weak as unsatisclisted farmers.
"These people only had minimal benefit from the surrender," Sok Leang of the Justice and Reconciliation Center, which runs public forums across Cambodia, including Anlong Vengu.
"They are surrounded by the utopian agrarian ideology of the regime."
"They were educated without any notions of making a trade."
Sor Lim, 55, who joined the Khmer Khmes as a teenager in 1974, fought in the jungle around Anlong Veng before settling as a poor farmer in 1998.
"Living under the Khmer Rouge was good," he said.
"That Mok sustained them all."
"But now life is hard, because we have to secure everything on our own."
On-governing Khmer Khmer Rouge court also raised fears.
Earlier next year, the court will begin investigating Nuon Chea, Khieu Samphan, Ieng Sandra and his wife, Ieng Thirith, another former Khmer Rouge minister.
The court has a mandate to prosecute leaders and people responsible for the crimes, but it has not been said whether it would also continue in the given four cases.
It wasn't enough to reassucate fears in Anlong Veng.
Relentful media speculation focused on Im Chaema, 64, who was a regional leader in Banteay Meanchey province during Khmer Rou era in the late 70's.
In 2007, she told researchers from the Documentation Center in Cambodia that she oversaw the construction of the Trapeang Thma dam, a project likely to kill thousands of forced workers.
A resuspite sweltering in an Evening, Im Chaem went back from work in the field into his wooden house on a filthy journey outside of Anlong Veng.
As the sausage began descent and shadows emerged over the aridgrased grasses, Im Chaem declined to talk about her past among the Khmer Khmeles.
If she were summoned, the court would refuse to go.
"There is peace ruled in Cambodia and it is stable," she said.
"If there are further prosecutions, there will be a war."
Prime Minister Hun Sen, who defected from Khmer Rouge in 1978 also repeatedly warned that there would be inconsistency if the court seeks other suspects, although experts and historians do not agree.
"Cambodia has to excavate a deep hole and bury the past in it," he said.
But the confrontation with the past is only what Cambodia has to do to move forward, Chhang Youk of the Documentation Center said.
"Acceptance in Khmer conditions means gluing broken pieces," he said.
"It is our duty to put these broken pieces together so that we can understand each other."
The center has published the first textbook approved by the Khmer Rouge government, it has 75 pages, and the title "History of Democratic Cambodia."
Distributed in Anlong Vengu in June as an addition to the history textbook of the Ministry of Education, which contains less than four parties about the Khmer Rouge.
Just like many people in Cambodia, young people in Anlong Vengo also knew only a few details about the Khmer Rouge, despite the connection of the city with the regime.
Touch Valeak, 19, a high-graduating student at Anlong Veng said the new textbook helps students understand an important part of their history.
But his parents reacted skeptically when he took home a book to study.
"My family denounces many parts of the textbook and the courts," he said.
"They are suspicious because they are unsure how many people the court will prosecute."
Such resistant makes the process of reconciliation more complicated and more difficult, Sok Leang said.
But public forums, textbooks and the court are beginning to have an influence, he said.
The Khmer-Differs still have a strong charm here.
On top of the Dangrek Mountains is a path overgrown with weeds and sprinkled with disguised plastic sausage, which leads to a rectangle made of black soot, covered with a rusty tin roof.
The body of Pol Pot was burnt here on a pile of tire tires after his death in 1998.
Nuom Sothea, 31, a cell phone saleswowoman on the outskirts of the road said she didn't know much about the man who was cremated in that place.
"But he has a strong spirit, a lot of local people are walking and praying towards him," she said.
Nuom Sothea had a birthday, and she was planning to later visit Pol Pota's resting place, where a bunch of ripe bananas would be brought in the hope that it would bring her luck.
Belarus Agrees That Uranium-based Weapon Settlements Appropriations
Speaking in President Obama's surprise vis-à-plateau campaign to secure nuclear material around the world, the Belarusian government announced on Wednesday that they would give up its reserves of highly-enhanced uranium, a critical component of nuclear weapons.
The treaty was published in a joint statement, issued after the security summit by Secretary of State Hillary Rodham Clinton and Belarusian Foreign Minister Sergei Martynov.
For years, the U.S U.S. government has been trying to persuade Belarus to give up highly-enriched uranium, and it appeared that they had encountered a stumbling block last spring.
But U.S. officials told The Washington Post that in the past two months, a pair of classified operations had been carried out with Belarus to remove 187 pound uranium-based weapons from a Belarusian research facility.
It paved the way for Wednesday's deal to remove the remnude of the material - approximately £500, according to an official who spoke on condition of an anonymity because it is a sensitive issue.
The agreement turned out to be one of Obama's 47th National Nuclear Security Summit in April, at which Belarus was eliminated.
And it was probably a good move to ensure the invitation of the former Soviet Republic to the follow-up summit in 2012.
"I was really fascinulsized by the fact that they wanted a place at a summit on nuclear security 2012."
"This proves that the process is working," said Ken Luongo, president of the Partnership for Global Security, groups supporting nuclear security.
Clinton has dubbed an agreement as "a very significant, important step" from Belarus.
Obama vowed in Prague in April 2009 to secure all sensitive nuclear materials around the world within the age of four.
Since then, the U.S. government has assisted six countries get rid of all its reserves of highly-enhanced uranium.
Bharuusia will be the seventh.
A docile country did so under the administration of George W. Bush.
At the time of Obama's Nuclear Safety Summit, Belarusian President Alexander Lukashenko announced that his nation will never give up its uranium.
"We have highly enriched uranium-hundred-hundred-hundred-pound pounds of what's basically the basis for a weapon and less enriched uranium," Lukashenko said.
"It's our commodity crude...We don't intend to produce dirty bombs, and we also don't want to sell it to anyone."
"We only use it for research purposes, that's all."
Belarus, however, a few months ago acceded to the United States and expressed an interest in reaching an agreement.
The first commitment to eliminate Belarusian uranium in October was particularly complicated because the material was lightly illuminated, officials announced.
It was loaded onto a special train that traveled more than 1,300 miles to the Russian facility for storage and liquidation, the officials said.
The second operation was closed on Monday and was carried out by aeronauad, officials said.
The U.S. government spent $13 million on the program, most of it for the supply to the Belarusian research institute less enriched uranium, which is not suitable for the production of nuclear weapons.
"Everything has been coordinated very carefully and managed on the basis of confidence", which led to an agreement signed by Clinton, said Andrew Bieniawski, an official of the Department of Energy working for the Global Threat Reduction.
This year, the James-Mart Martin Center for Numetal Non-Proliferation estimated that Belarus has at least 88 pound uranium applicable to the production of weapons.
According to a joint statement, Belarus will seek to remove its stocks by 2012.
Relations between Belarus and the United States have been tensed for years, mainly due to Lukashenko's cruel human rights repression.
But Wednesday's statement indicates the possibility of gradual thrift.
"Welcome to the progress on these global security issues. The United States and Belarus have acknowledged that more consistent respect for democracy and human rights in Belarus remains the basis for improving mutual relations and it is necessary for the progress of the country and its people," he said.
