Red Bull triumphs in Formula 1 with secret weapon from Siemens
The software proposes improvements in vehicles in real-of-in-time.
Ever since Red Bull crashed into Formula 1 in 2004, its history is characterized by surprise and success.
At the beginning, no one took too seriously the possibilities of this newcomer team, and despite the upward trend from recent times, everyone was beheading Fernand Alonso's Ferrari triumph.
Without wanting to reduce the equestrian abilities of the champagne, German Sebastian Vettel, or the capabilities of a Renault engine that drives his chariot, in the world of Formula 1 a legend - a "secret weapon" that multi-National Siemens put in the hands of the Red Bull was born in order to make it a winning team.
In fact, it is a powerful software simulating marches, which the German company put on the market in 2007 for very distant automobile sports.
The so-called PLM, this powerful software device, was used to design assembly lines in production plants around the world, in power plants and for high-in-efficiency devices.
Its aim is to faithfully reproduce the product or to simulate the process before it is realistically created, thereby avoiding unnecessary errors and expenses.
PLM has made it easier to develop a Canon camera, Sukhoi aircraft or Nissan’s most famous commercial vehicles.
Siegfried Russwurm, head of the Siemens industry division, explains that the use of this software device has allowed the Red Bull team to carry out "after a few hours, in near-re real-of-mortgage" modifications and enhancements that are otherwise detected during races and rehearsals.
Russwurm says that in 1992 the stables occupied an average of 10 technicians who were able to carry out a maximum of three or four improvements over a single period.
The Red Bull team now employs 180 young highly-skilled high-profile information-efficiencies who are analysing and designing every type of racing vehicle modification.
Siemens leader explained that PLM software is capable of analyzing and upgrading all elements of Red Bull racing vehicles, with the exception of an engine that Renault is fully accounted for.
Modify materials, fuel, aerodynamics and safety features.
The changes can be converted from virtual reality into reality in less than 24 hours thanks to advanced production systems using laser and highly technologically advanced autoclaves.
The only limitation of this technical achievement is the $40 million that each Formula 1 team can spend for each period to improve its competitiveness.
The Red Bull has set up a high-profile plant of cutting-edge technologies to design and produce its racing cars about 50 km from London.
These are the two buildings that are limited by the entrance of visits - especially to journalists.
Everything that takes place here is considered a "great secret".
580 people, including two pilots - champion Vettel and Mark Webber - the list of these projects include 580 people.
The hero of the Red Bull in Formula 1 without a doubt is a fresh champion Sebastian Vettel.
Behind the immeasurable technological means that the brand of energetic beverages has put into its participation in the racing circuits hide other protagonists whose help is invaluable, while no one knows them.
The head of production technology Steve Nevey argues that five years ago it was possible to say that advances in the research and development of the vehicles contributed to a 50% victory and the remainder was due to the drivers.
At the same time, this ratio has changed, and the technology is behind 85%, while the pilots contribute 15%.
Nevey recognizes Vettel or Webber will never be replaced by computer technology because they are "unpredictable gladiators".
