CECA excludes another wave of fusion societies
CECA's chief executive denied yesterday that there should be another wave of savings-movie mergers, and once again insisted on the speed with which the refurbishment of the sector is being reorganised.
Taking it off when the Spanish Savings Allo-Association process began, there were several institutions that assured that they would not participate in this first phase and would wait for the second round.
The director-in-general of the Spanish Confederation of Saviour (CECA) José Antonio Olavarrieta yesterday ruled that a new wave of these operations should be created.
"I believe that most of the mergers have already been carried out, Cajasol's incorporation into Banca Cívica can not be considered a second round, what is taking place are conventions".
“Majority is completely executed; it is possible that instead of 17 constitutions we will go to 13 or 14, but all the great processes are already completed and under the protection of the Spanish National Bank, which has cooperated very well in this matter,” he said.
Speaking in his statements at the AREA (Association of Heads and Executive Officials of Aragon). Olavarrieta recounted that the process of concentration takes place "at the highest possible speed and we have to be very satisfied, as shown in in installments, which at the same time, not now, have emerged in business plans with the Spanish National Bank," he stressed. He stressed.
“Following 45 savellibles with an average asset of $28,000 million at $75,500 million is a process that does not have a comparison in history, reducing the number of institutions by 62 per cent to make them larger and thus in accordance with the Treaty of Basel,” he underlined.
Olavarrieta has acknowledged that "there is a number of not-so-pleasant views on the slowness of the process" that show "the lack of seriousness and sympathy" because "What the Spanish National Bank does is to recall that we have to be on date before the end of the year".
CECA CEO José Antonio Olavarrieta reasspected yesterday that Spain has the strictest financial system in all of Europe and "probably" in the world, and to those who question it were advocating an "lack of knowledge".
The director noted that in the current favorable economic situation "we can be calm" about the financial institutions and the Saviors, as well as a system that has handled a "very very important" process of concentration and which continues "the highest possible speed".
He also recalled that Spanish banking emerged very well from the stress tests carried out by the European Central Bank in July.
Zapatero and the Red German Line
Berlin has opted to maintain the euro, but has set conditions for the whole Union.
Tensions in the markets provoked a two-time government response.
On the one hand, José Luis Zapatero proposed new adaptation measures, from tax cuts for small and medium-sized enterprises, through the regulation of private intermediary agencies or the abolition of €426 for unemployment excluding benefits, to the privatization of 49% AENA (Spanish airports and air traffic control), airport administrator Barajas and El Prat and 30% of the state lottery.
Vice-president Elena Salgado with the support of former Prime Minister Felip González, on the other hand, has joined the criticism of Chancellor Angela Merkel because of her responsibility to unleash the current crisis.
How to Interpret These Shields?
It should be said in advance that Merkel's position is not a mistake in her communication policy.
On the other hand, it is another point in a complex euro redefinition process.
And here Germany radically has a new vision of what its future should be, which alludes to the ideological adhesions of old traditions that a large proportion of other members hold.
In the face of European resistance, there is beginning to speculate in Germany about three possible paths from the crisis.
The first represents a strong eurozone, of which it would include Germany, as well as Austria and the Netherlands, while the southern states would leave the monetary zone.
The second is the return to the mark - although it may now appear as sci-fi, this version has the broad support of the public, approaching 50% of the population.
The third option, according to the German proposals for the reproclubration of the euro with greater macroeconomic oversuaservants, budgetary rigour, fines for guilty states, is the possible depreciation of payments for some of them, and the extension of possible regulatory costs for the private sector.
The German position is clear and has already been defined for some time, so there is now no room for equators.
Earlier, when the International Monetary Fund and the European Commission fueled deficits in 2009, the German government pulled a constitutional reform out of its sleeve in order to prevent them since 2016.
This was the first sign of the contradiction between what Germany thought was right (share erecbilities and stability of public finances) and what was practiced by its European partners, including France, had practiced.
This Central European country showed the second when Merkel painfully protracted decisions regarding the bailout of Greece.
At the same time it was the subject of sharp criticism, but the truth is that the political consensuity in Germany and public opinion, which was also published, were strongly opposed to the bailout of Greece.
Finally, in the last days of October, Merkel reapplied with a clear position, calling for European Union conventions so as to allow rescuing a Member State and, at the same time, that private creditors have an obligation to bear part of the costs.
This opinion was interpreted as anti-European because it accelerated the Irish crisis and everything else.
However, its suggestion, albeit somewhat mitigated form, has been approved, and over the coming months we should monitor the reforming process of the Union, which should enable these measures from 2013.
Germany has laid out a red line, which it does not intend to cross.
And he informs all of us that, although we now need to take measures that they do not seem to confluge too much with, from this date will be the end with the old functioning of the euro.
The year 2013 will represent the birth of a new currency zone in which states violating the rules will be sanctioned hard-to-sanction and may be forced to depreciate the depreciation of payments, and the private sector will also have to bear part of the cost.
Germany is suggesting to us that we need to take a staggering path, with an increase in the rate of savings and investment, moderate consumption, improvement of productivity and competitiveness, and capital reserves.
Another option based on receivables and rebuild, with enormous gaps in the balance of payments ruled over the years of Spain's growth boom, simply does not intend to tolerate.
The offer is clear.
We'd make a mistake if we didn't understand.
It’s not just about restoring the public deficit to 3% in 2013.
It's a lot harder they want us to do.
And the worst thing is, under these conditions, it could happen to us that we are fooling ourselves.
I'm hoping that these current turbulence will be more peaceful.
And that we will take advantage of the opportunity they are giving us until the spring of 2013.
Prime Minister Zapatero also seems to have grasped this, and his double statements (reforms pledged after a meeting with major businesses from last Saturday and yesterday's announcement of the new measures) gives an idea that, despite critics to Merkel, the path of the idea that things need to be put in order have been opened.
However, the red line that Germany has set is here, and it doesn’t seem to want to move it.
One of the possibilities that can be considered cannot be ruled out leaving the Eurozone full of unsecured non-serious countries.
Well, Germany will always be able to tell us that it has warned us.
Sport and circumcission of the rules
Finty, such as Alonso and Ramos in a match against Ajax, show wit not the intent to escape the punishment.
An allegedly orchestrated excluding two Real Madrid players (Xabi Alons and Sergio Ramos) in the Champions League against Ajax on November 23 has caused a sea of ink to be written about whether or not unsportsmanlike conduct, and whether UEFA shouldn't be punished by both footballers.
As it is known, a few minutes before the end of the match (who their team had clearly won), the appointed players deliberately lost time one by the moment the foul was supposed to play in order to provoke the referee, who showed them a second yellow card.
In this way, the two players will fulfil the prescribed penalty in the following match against Auxerre - while Real Madrid will end up in their group at the forefront - and they will be able, deprived of their cards, to the next stage.
In the end, the UEFA Disciplinary Committee gave coach Jose Mourinho a two-match penalty, and the guilty players were fine.
Provocating a yellow card under similar conditions is not a new practice, but it is more or less common in football.
It's so pregnant that it's part of an ethos of football, that is, rules that are unepersed, but are being watched.
Part of the discussion in this case arose thanks to the fact that its participant was the Real Madrid team (who in its anthem boasts generosity and honor, and which has already finedied its players for similar acts), and it seems, also thanks to the dramatization led by Mourinho, which thus adds another episode to its long history of the comedian and provocateur.
in any case, the debate revolves around whether this kind of conduct, which is not expressly prohibited by the rules, is contrary to the principles of sport, i.e. whether these deliberations are circumcispid the law; these are indeed actions respecting sporting rules, the meaning of which, of course, they seem to try to circumnavigate.
It solves what kind of damage such behavior such conduct acts in a sporting spirit.
One of the requisites of sporting behavior is the physical inviolability of opposing players, which has not been violated in these cases, i.e. there was no forcible play.
Another of these requisites characterizing a clean game is to avoid players from having an advantage over the opponent's team due to their infraction - which didn't happen here as well.
Thus, the paradoxical event of the Real Madrid players is the fact that there was no such thing that we normally characterize as a guinea fowl or circumcisive of the law, there has not been a cover-of-file violation of the rules in order to escalate the sanctuation, but this behavior should be as evident as much as possible in order to receive the punishment.
They waited and they were happy to be punished.
However, the next question is what advantage the culprits of their deliberate offense will have.
Here it is necessary to state that the opponent does not get into a disadvantaged position, and that the current match does not achieve any benefit, because the obvious consequence is that the team itself will have fewer players.
And even this is not a useful strategy for the next match, because the club will not be able to count on these players.
An advisable is that the players will begin the next phase of the Champions League without cards, which could jeopardize their participation in a future match.
However, it is an advantage from a long-term prospect and a certain degree of indetermination, i.e. at first glance, the team can be eliminated, in addition to this, players will be punished, and they will go to the second round of cards, in which one less card to be expelled than in the first round.
So as the rounds will continue, it won't be entirely clear if it's an advantage.
In short, these negotiations do not go against the sporting spirit, of which it is a part to make strategic decisions, which will make it possible to obtain the utmost benefit within the rules.
in the case that we are dealing with here, the players did not aim to evacuate the penalty (which would be a clear case of circumvention of the rules), but rather they chose when to substantiate it.
They made a strategic decision without escalating or fleeing punishment.
Although the boundary is not quite clear between wit and circumvention of the rules, I believe it was unequivocally savvy.
It seems to me that controversy arises for two reasons: because it was two self-exclusion and because of the way in which the transmission of instructions was carried out to provoke them from the leadership.
Damage of sporting principles by these events is not as ethical as aesthetic.
