Obama Promotes Law on Twitter to Promosent-Evidence-Undocumented Students
"If you believe that the passage of the Dream Act is correct, let your senators know and ask them to pass this bill," the U.S. president tweeted.
President Barack Obama today, with a post on his personal account of the social networking site Twitter, connected to a national mobilization in support of the Dream Act, which should legalize students without documents.
“While you believe that the passage of the Dream Act is right, let your senators know and ask them to pass this bill,” Obama tweeted.
His short report draws attention to an online link to a more comprehensive document asking the public to contact senators without delay, as voting on this bill is approaching.
"Suither it is anticipated that the vote will take place soon, it is now a moment to let your opinion be expressed," he points out.
The report was released after 42 Republican senators threatened to sanctify the passage of any law unless an extension is approved for the Bush era tax cut and government funding.
Such lockdown, which was announced by a letter signed by 42 Republican senators, could influence a vote on the Dream Act and other projects, such as ratification of a new treaty with Russia against the proliferation of nuclear-weapon prognosis, START.
Responsible when he has been questioned about the Republican blocking of the Dream Act and other proposals, the presidential spokeswoman said he believed that once the fiscal issue has been resolved, it would be possible to draw up a calendar for the rest of the projects.
"I'm sure there's still plenty of time left..."
"We all agree that we have to deal with a matter of taxes before the end of the year..."
I believe that we will be able to reach an agreement in this matter at some point and then we will be able to plan the order of other matters," he said.
Senate Democratic Majority leader Harry Reid announced on Tuesday that he would seek "vote to conclude a discussion" regarding the Dream Act, so a proposal to legalize undocumented students could be voted on in the senate from next Thursday, although it is more presumably next week.
Seniary approval of the Dream Act, or the "American Dream Act", would be legalized by about 800,000 young people who came to the United States before the age of 16, stayed in the country over the past five years and have been admitted to an institution of higher education or the military.
However, supporters need to applauntain 60 votes so they need the help of several Republicans, so the organization of its supporters are carrying out an intensive agitation to ensure the passage of this bill.
Democrats, whose most Dream Act supports, have 58 mandates.
Nevertheless, at least one of them, Senator Ben Nelson of Nebraska, has stated that he will vote, and only one Republican, Richard Lugar of Indiana, expressed his support for the law.
That's why adherents of the Dream Act need to recruit several Republicans in the event that other democrats choose to reject the proposal.
Among the Republican senators with whom the supporters are most negotiated, Maine senators Olympia Snow and Susan Colins, as well as Florida outgoing senator George Lemieux, who supplemented Senator Mel Martínez's mandate.
Other Republicans who supported the Dream Act in the past are Orrin Hatch from Utah and Kay Bailey Hutchison of Texas, but they said they wouldn't support it next time.
Another Republican who supported the proposal, John Mcain, has not yet commented at this time.
Although the Chamber of Adversaries is inclined towards a different version, according to most analysts, the Dream Act will face the biggest huddges in the senate.
