World Plan to Rebuild Lighting
The U.N. yesterday in Cancún proposed a plan to rebuild domestic illumination, lighting shops and industrial premises in 100 countries with the aim of achieving restrictions on the greenhouse effect (GES), equivalent to nearly one-three additional restrictions that need to be achieved to stabilise the planet's climatic climate from now until 2020.
This Ambitious plan is at a stage of large-controversial projects that the United Nations wants to push through at Cancún to offsetting the lack of hard-to-floquity commitments to reduce on the side of industrialized countries.
According to the plan presented yesterday, if hot filaments, which monopolize 50- to 70% of the market, are replaced by flu-compectic or, even better, light bulbs with electroluminescent diode (DEL or "LED" bulbs in English), a substantial amount of 1.8 billion tons (Gt) GES emissions on the planet due to illumination would be degraduous.
Illumination is accountable for 8% of the world's emissions.
It's the equivalent of 70% emissions from an automobile park all over the planet.
In Kodani last year, large countries with a significant share of emissions such as the United States, China, Europe and others, among them Canada, engaged in voluntary reduction plans, which are expected to reduce global GES emissions from 53 Gt to 49 Gt.
But it will have to be subdivided from this balance of 5 Gt in order to achieve climate stabilization to 2 degrees Celsius by 2020 in order to exclude that it does not get out of control.
In hot bulbs 95% of the energy is dissipated in the form of heat and the remainder, i.e. 5%, is the product of light.
With flu-compective incandescent incandescent bulbs, the energy consumption is reduced by 75% and with new incandescent bulbs with DEL, we will reduce this consumption twice more.
Flu-compact bulbs can withstand on average from 6000 to 10 000 hours.
New incandescent incandescent bulbs with DEL, such, which this week put into the production of Philips - this company allegedly participated in the UN study - have an expected lifespan of 25 000 hours and consume 80% less energy than a hot bulb, giving us an idea of possible savings with a replacement plan that would be injected in 100 countries.
All these new light bulbs can also work with rheotus and exute less heat.
They contain no mercury.
Several experts have expressed objections to flu-compective bulbs because they contain mercury and need to be sorted as hazardous waste, which is still not possible in developing countries, unless manufacturers set up collection centres.
However, as the report states, the main source of mercury emissions on the planet isn’t a flu-compact filament lamp, but rather thermal coal-fired power plants that emit the many mercets needed to generate electricity wasted in hot incandescent bulbs.
In the U.S. alone, these centers put 25 000 tons of mercury into circulation every year.
The report also outlines in detail the profits every country from this international programme.
For example, Indonesia would thus save a billion dollars a year for the purchase of electricity and eight million tons of CO2, the equivalent of a 3.5-firer power-fired power plant whose construction would cost $2.5 billion
