Revenge Hackers and Difficult Spies
WikiLeaks case has a dual meaning.
On the one hand, an obvious scandal is revealed, a scandal that appears to be a scandal only in the face of hypocrisy governing relations between states, citizens and the press.
On the other hand, it talks about deep changes at the international level and anticipates the future dominated by regression.
But let's proceed in order.
WikiLeak’s first aspect is a confirmation of the fact that each document compiled by a secret service (of any nation) consists entirely of press cuts.
“Extreme Revelation” by Americans about the way Berlusconi’s sexual life is announcing what everyone has been able to read for months in any of the newspapers (everything but these two Berlusconi owns), and Gadafi’s indisadvantly cartoutory profile has long been a theme for sketches.
The rule, according to which classified documents may consist only of messages already known, is essential in the dynamics of the secret services, and not only in this century.
When you go to a bookstore devoted to esoteric literature, you will see that each work repeats (about Graal, the mystery of Rennes-le-Château, the Templar or the Masons) exactly what has already been written in previous works.
And this is not only because the author of occult texts does not like to search for new information (wrong doesn’t even know where to look for non-existent news), but because those engaged in occluctism believe only what they already know and what confirms what they have previously learned.
That's the mechanism of Dan Brown's accomplishment.
The same goes for secret documents.
The whistleblower is lazy, and a lazy (or limited spirit) the head of the secret services (and if he isn't, he could, as I do I know, an editor in Libération newspapers), who only finds true what he himself admits.
High-secured information about Berlusconi, which the U.S. embigreement sent to the state department, was the same that Newsweek had published the week before.
So why was there so much fuss about revealing these documents?
On the one hand, they say what every cultivated person already knows, namely that the embassies, at least from the end of World War II, and since the heads of state can call on or depart by plane for a joint dinner, they have lost their diplomatic function and, with the exception of a few small exercises in representation, they have been transformed into spy centers.
Any spectator who watches about the investigation knows very well the fact that it's seemingly ignominded out of liquority.
Nevertheless, the fact that it repeats itself in the public disgraces the duty of hypocrisy and casts a bad light on U.S. diplomacy.
On the other hand, the idea that any hacker can get the most closely classified information of the world's mightiest country represents a hard blow, not insignificant for the prestige of this state-of-the-art ministry.
The scandal affects both the victims and its "educators".
But let's go from here to the deep truth about what happened.
Once upon a time, in the times of Orwell it was possible to conceive all the power, like some Big Brother, who controlled every movement of his people.
Orvell's prophecy has been proven ever since, thanks to the possibility of controlling every movement thanks to the phone, every transaction carried out, the hotel visited, the highway used and so on, the citizen has become an utter victim of a wretch of power.
But when we are demonstrating how it goes now that even "threeks" with the most comprehensive information of the powerful can not resist the intervention of a hacker, the control message ceases to be one-way and becomes a circular.
Power is controlled by every citizen, but every citizen, or at least a hacker - chosen as an avenucher of a citizen - can know all the secrets of power.
How can a power that no longer has the opportunity to preserve its own secrets?
It's true that Georg Simmel has already said that a true secret is an inconspicuous secrecy (and an insuffimitic secrecy can never be revealed); it's also true that all information about Berlusconi or Merkel's character is indeed a mysterious unattachable secret because it's pulled out of public life; but to reveal as WikiLeaks has done, that Hillary Clinton's secret has been unabstracted.
WikiLeaks didn’t make a mistake with Sarkozy or Merkel, but he did something great to Clinton and Obama.
What will be the consequences of this plague-tained power so significant?
It is clear that in the future states will not be able to send on-line sensitive information - this would be tantamount to its publication on a poster glued to a street corner.
But it is also quite obvious that with current technologies, it is useless to hope that it is possible to keep secregated messages over the phone.
There's nothing easier than figuring out if and when the head of state traveled on a plane and got in touch with one of his colleagues.
In the future, how will they be able to be kept under secrecy private and sensible messages in the future?
I'm well aware that for the moment my prognosis belongs to science-fiction and so it's fantastic, but I have to imagine government officials being transported discrepcessive to perform formalities on uncontrollable routes and carry only those messages that they know by heart or rather hide rare information in the heel of shoes.
The information will be retained in unparalleled-closed-securement copies: in total, an attempt at spying in the Watergate affair had less success than WikiLeaks.
I've had the opportunity to write that the technology is now moving side-by-fly, that's going backwards.
One century, when a wireless telegraph marked a revolution in communications, the Internet rehabilitated a telegraph based on wire (telephonic).
Video cassettes (analogues) have allowed cinema lovers to shoot the film step by step, move it forward and backward, and reveal all the secrets of montage, while the CD (number-to-nuclear) now only allows you to jump on chapters, i.e. on macro portions.
Through high-speed trains, we will arrive from Rome to Milan in three hours, while traveling by plane and transportation, which includes we need three-and-a-half an hour.
Therefore, it is unfamiliatory that politics and communication techniques will return to horse-drawed cars.
