Clima: how France hacked its funding vows
A year after the Copenhagen agreement, it is time to test compliance with the promises of quickly unblocked financing (meablished fast-term financing (fast start) or premature), implemented by developed countries.
And big French hacks have been uncovered
In the Kodano Agreement, the developed countries pledged to provide $30 billion in 2010-2012.
The European Union has pledged €7.2 billion for three years, and France €1.26 billion (that's 420 million per annum).
At the start of COP16, the EU pulled out a large ace to convince its partners about the reality of its fast start financing commitment: a report specifying the actions of its Member States, a press conference, a grand side event, the distribution of brochure brochures...
Transparent operation invaluable and appreciated at its true value: developed countries and civil society would like all developed countries and civil society to do them the same and with the same degree of accuracy.
The EU is announcing €2.2 billion vacuate in 2010, bringing it to the right path to meeting its promise of $7.2 billion in 2012.
But if we focus on the detail, we will find that things are much more complicated.
According to the Copenhagen Accord, these funds are "new and additional" in order to ensure that an increased burden is realised, without harming Public aid for development.
Unfortunately, there is no consensus on what "new and additional" means.
While the European Commission has proposed harmonization, some Member States, among others France, have blocked the process.
Today everyone chooses the reference that suits them best.
The European Commission, for example, considered it a reference year 2009: international funding has been preserved and it adds 150 million for three years of fast-tasting funding.
Other countries have chosen similar references with annual payment deadlines or deadlines at the level of public aid for development.
On the other hand, France believes that the financing of "all new projects" in 2010 is "new and additional".
In other words, there is no reference to the expenditures of previous periods or to the Global Level of Public Assistance for the development of d'Aide publique au développement.
In specific terms, the total amount of support of France vis-advised countries may remain immutable (or even reduced), it is intended to comply with its commitment to the financing of "new and additional "...
Another important element is the division into donations and loans.
There doesn't already be a rule and everyone chooses their own method of calculation.
Some, such as France, contemplate that 1€ borrowed is equal to 1€ donated.
Evidently, it is difficult to prove: an effort is not comparable.
If the use of loans can be substantiated for certain investments that are profitable (often projects associated with reducing emissions consisting of more efficient technologies), it is foolish to present the one euro that we get, as the equivalent of the one euro that we are giving..., we shouldn't understand it by virtue of our liabilities, but as the price of a loan for a lending country (i.e. the equivalent of an interest rate).
But France can only give such a justification: it "observes" its €420 million liability, of which 91.5% makes a loan (389.8 million euros) and 8.5% donations (€36.1 million) - see the EU document so that you can verify it yourself.
It is the only country in the Union that uses a loan on such a scale.
Germany has demanded 46% from there, while Denmark, the Netherlands and Slovakia (between others) comply with their obligations with 100% donations.
In other words, when the vast majority of the European Union gives 1 €1 in the context of satisfying its promise, France will give only 10 centimeters and consider this effort to be comparable.
Beautiful juggling piece!
For summbermation, in its "fast start" financing, France operates with a double scelerism: on the one hand with the development of a stock over time and, on the other hand, with a real financial burden.
Although it spends just €36.1 million, it can concise that it spends €420 million "new and additional" in connection with its promises.
Manipulation of the finance minister (since it seems that although Bercy, who made such decisions) could have a double negative impact, at the same time within the EU (why would the member states continue the donation when it is enough just to borrow?) and in the negotiations, because developing countries are not entirely naive...
Premature Financing Chapter is only a small question, a relatively short-lived, in relation to a series of climate discussions.
But it could also be a grain of sand in a huge machine such as UNFCCC.
Let's hope this isn't our case.
Whatever it is, it is impertinent to draw knowledge from this first year of early financing.
At first, France should announce a change in its way of keeping accounting records for the years 2011 and 2012, in the spirit of observing its commitments and decency and fairness to other countries of the European Union.
But above all, France should avoid a discussion on deposits in long-term financing (another fundamental chapter currently discussed): we will not escape the question of "additionality" or the method of posting loans.
“Fewards should change its stance in order to become as progressive for these deposits as for others.
Therefore, everyone puts the hope into the new Minister of the Environment, Mrs Koscius-Morizet, who will come to Cancun next week.
Surprising announcement of the fast launch of France and its vision of "additionality" and the utilisation of loans could provoke an increase in the level of international confidence, necessary in order to move forward in discussions.
An alarm on pesticides that children devour.
A ten-year-old devours 81 different chemicals on a day, according to one study published this Wednesday, from the Future Generations Association (ex- Movements for Rights and Conservation of Future Generations).
The association, which points out that its results "do not have a significant statistical value with regard to the low number of samples analysed, but are characterised by the issues being processed", bought between July and September foods commonly consumed in four meals and one slight meal as a slight sausage per child of about a ten-year-old in supermarkets in l'Oise and in Paris.
These typical dishes, including official recommendations - five pieces of fresh fruits and vegetables, three dairy products and 1.5 liters of water a day - as well as confectionery, have been analyzed by research laboratories for chemicals, pesticides, heavy metals and other polluting agents.
The balance of the plate is crushing: 81 chemicals, of which 42 are cartinogenic, possible or probable, and five substances are among the clear carcinogenic substances, as well as 37 substances that could act as intrusive for internal excretions (PE), according to studies by several institutions, such as the International Centre for Cancer Research.
"While in almost all cases the legal limits for each chemical received individually are not exceeded, in our research we can well see that the reality in consumer exposure to contaminating substances, perhaps carcinogenic, and/or PE is startling," the association said.
For breakfast butter and tea with milk contain on their own about ten respirations with possible carcinogenic effects and three proven as certain carcinogens, such as about twenty lesions that could disrupt the hormonal system.
An apple, imported from Brazil, cited as a small snack, contains traces of six chemical substances, among other things, a banned fungicidal agent in France.
The same goes for green beans from Kenya, included in lunch, for which the studio has discovered traces of strong insecticides banned in the EU.
In addition, biffek from minced meat, tuna in a can and even a bread and chewing chewing chewing were riddled with pesticides and other chemicals.
Analyses revealed the presence of nitrates and Chloroform.
Steak from suspense to supervis was "the "richest" with 34 discovered chemical residuals, and even analysing a plastic plate used to re-heat food in a microwave didn't avoid the analysis.
The final risk to the consumers of this "cock of contaminants" is likely to be underestimated", according to a study.
Eco-Wood-Collabitation (EE-LV) has proposed a "face-in-faith of an urgent situation" to set up a moratorium on the use of pesticides to halt an "increase of civilizational diseases."
In July, a study by the European Food Safety Agency (EFSA) surveyed the state of the presence of residues of 365 different pesticides identified in fruits and vegetables consumed in Europe, of which 76 in cereal.
